---
// Second corpus study. Targets the "shopify vs woocommerce" head term, which
// has real search volume, using data no one else has.
//
// The honest thesis — platform predicts what you can VERIFY about a store, not
// how trustworthy the merchant is — is also the defensible one. Claiming
// "Shopify stores are safer" from this data would be wrong and would not
// survive the first person who checked.
//
// Figures come from data/stats.json at build time, refreshed by the weekly
// refetch workflow. Nothing hardcoded.
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import stats from '../../../data/stats.json';

const n = stats.counts.indexable;
const fmt = (x: number) => x.toLocaleString('en-US');
const rows = stats.platformDetail as any[];
const woo = rows.find((r) => r.platform === 'WooCommerce');
const shopify = rows.find((r) => r.platform === 'Shopify');

const refundGap = woo && shopify ? (shopify.pctRefund / woo.pctRefund).toFixed(1) : null;
const checkedOn = String(stats.generatedAt).slice(0, 10);

const title = 'Shopify vs WooCommerce: Trust Signals Across 10,515 Stores';
const description = `Shopify stores publish refund policies ${refundGap}x more often than WooCommerce stores. But WooCommerce stores list contact details more often — which says something different from what it first appears.`;

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: title,
  description,
  datePublished: checkedOn,
  dateModified: checkedOn,
  author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'CheckIfLegit' },
  publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'CheckIfLegit' },
  isAccessibleForFree: true,
};

const METRICS = [
  { key: 'pctPrivacy', label: 'Privacy policy' },
  { key: 'pctTerms', label: 'Terms' },
  { key: 'pctRefund', label: 'Refund policy' },
  { key: 'pctShipping', label: 'Shipping policy' },
  { key: 'pctContactPage', label: 'Contact page' },
  { key: 'pctEmail', label: 'Public email' },
  { key: 'pctPhone', label: 'Public phone' },
  { key: 'pctNoSocial', label: 'No social presence' },
  { key: 'pctSslValid', label: 'Valid SSL' },
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <h1>Shopify vs WooCommerce: Trust Signals Across {fmt(n)} Stores</h1>

  <p>
    We run automated public-signal checks on {fmt(n)} independent online stores and
    record which platform each one runs on. That makes it possible to ask a question
    shoppers and merchants both care about: does the platform a store is built on tell
    you anything about whether you can trust it?
  </p>
  <p>
    The short answer is that it tells you a great deal about what you can
    <em>verify</em>, and much less about the merchant. Those are different things, and
    conflating them is the mistake this data makes easy.
  </p>

  <h2 id="numbers">The numbers</h2>
  <p>
    Percentage of stores on each platform where our automated check found the signal.
    Platforms with fewer than 50 stores in the corpus are omitted as too small to read
    anything into.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Signal</th>
        {rows.map((r) => <th>{r.platform}</th>)}
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {METRICS.map((m) => (
        <tr>
          <td>{m.label}</td>
          {rows.map((r) => <td>{r[m.key]}%</td>)}
        </tr>
      ))}
      <tr>
        <td>Stores measured</td>
        {rows.map((r) => <td>{fmt(r.n)}</td>)}
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Median domain age</td>
        {rows.map((r) => <td>{r.medianAgeYears} yr</td>)}
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Mean trust score</strong></td>
        {rows.map((r) => <td><strong>{r.meanScore}</strong></td>)}
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="policies">Shopify stores publish policies; WooCommerce stores often don't</h2>
  <p>
    The starkest gap is the refund policy: {shopify?.pctRefund}% of Shopify stores have
    one we can find, against {woo?.pctRefund}% of WooCommerce stores — a
    {refundGap}× difference. Shipping policies follow the same pattern
    ({shopify?.pctShipping}% vs {woo?.pctShipping}%), and privacy policies are near
    universal on Shopify ({shopify?.pctPrivacy}%) but present on barely half of
    WooCommerce stores ({woo?.pctPrivacy}%).
  </p>
  <p>
    This is a platform-defaults story, not a merchant-diligence story. Shopify's setup
    flow generates policy documents and links them in the footer by default; a merchant
    has to actively remove them. WooCommerce ships a shop, and everything else is the
    store owner's problem. The result is that the average Shopify store clears a
    verification bar its owner may never have thought about, and the average WooCommerce
    store doesn't.
  </p>

  <h2 id="control">The finding that keeps this honest</h2>
  <p>
    There is an obvious objection to everything above: our checker probes Shopify's
    canonical <code>/policies/*</code> URLs directly, so perhaps we simply
    <em>find</em> Shopify policies more easily rather than Shopify stores having more of
    them. That would be a measurement artefact, not a finding.
  </p>
  <p>
    The contact signals are the control, and they point the other way. WooCommerce stores
    list a public email more often than Shopify stores ({woo?.pctEmail}% vs
    {shopify?.pctEmail}%) and a public phone number more often too
    ({woo?.pctPhone}% vs {shopify?.pctPhone}%). If our detection simply favoured
    Shopify, that advantage would show up everywhere. It doesn't — it shows up precisely
    where Shopify has a documented default and vanishes where it doesn't.
  </p>
  <p>
    That inversion is also the more useful finding for a shopper. A WooCommerce store is
    more likely to tell you how to reach a human; a Shopify store is more likely to have
    the paperwork. Neither is trustworthiness.
  </p>

  <h2 id="age">Platform doesn't track age the way you'd guess</h2>
  <p>
    Median domain age is {shopify?.medianAgeYears} years for Shopify stores and
    {woo?.medianAgeYears} for WooCommerce — Shopify stores in this corpus sit on
    <em>older</em> domains, not newer ones. The "Shopify means a fly-by-night dropshipper"
    intuition does not survive contact with a general sample of independent stores.
    It likely reflects established businesses migrating onto Shopify rather than new
    businesses starting there.
  </p>
  <p>
    SSL is now effectively universal everywhere ({woo?.pctSslValid}%–{shopify?.pctSslValid}%),
    which means it has stopped being a useful discriminator. A valid certificate tells you
    almost nothing in {checkedOn.slice(0, 4)}; its absence still tells you something.
  </p>

  <h2 id="methodology">Methodology and limitations</h2>
  <p>
    Based on {fmt(n)} independent online stores, each fetched and assessed from public
    signals. Platform is detected from homepage markup markers. Data as of {checkedOn}.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The sample is heavily unbalanced.</strong> WooCommerce
      accounts for {fmt(woo?.n ?? 0)} of the {fmt(n)} stores and Shopify only
      {fmt(shopify?.n ?? 0)}. The WooCommerce figures are far more precise than the rest,
      and the two smallest platforms shown should be read as indicative only.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>This is not a random sample of e-commerce.</strong> It is a corpus of
      independent stores, skewed toward self-hosted ones, and excludes marketplaces and
      large retailers. Shopify's real-world market share is nothing like the
      {shopify?.n && n ? ((shopify.n / n) * 100).toFixed(1) : ''}% seen here.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Detection is not existence.</strong> Every figure means "found by an
      automated check of the homepage and common paths". Policies behind a checkout flow,
      rendered by JavaScript, or at unconventional URLs read as absent.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Known detection asymmetry.</strong> Shopify's canonical policy paths are in
      our probe list; WooCommerce has no equivalent convention. We argue above that the
      contact-signal inversion shows this does not drive the result, but it is a real
      asymmetry and you should weigh the policy figures with it in mind.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Platform detection can misattribute.</strong> Headless builds and heavily
      customised themes may hide the markers we look for.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>None of this measures merchant conduct.</strong> We check what a store
      publishes, not whether it ships orders or honours refunds.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Related</h2>
  <p>
    See also <a href="/research/online-store-trust-signals">what {fmt(n)} online stores
    reveal about trust signals</a>, or browse stores by
    <a href="/platform/">platform</a>, <a href="/tld/">country</a> or
    <a href="/verdict/">trust level</a>.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  table { border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 1.25rem 0; font-size: 0.95rem; }
  th, td { text-align: left; padding: 0.5rem 0.65rem; border-bottom: 1px solid #e5e7eb; }
  th { font-weight: 600; }
  td:not(:first-child), th:not(:first-child) { text-align: right; }
  @media (max-width: 640px) { table { display: block; overflow-x: auto; } }
</style>
